[[File:2025 Cook PVI.svg|thumb|360px|Map of [[2020 United States presidential election|2020]]–[[2024 United States presidential election|2024]] Cook PVI for all voting entities in the [[2028 United States presidential election]] ([[United States state|states]], [[District of Columbia|federal district]], [[list of United States congressional districts|congressional districts]] of [[Maine]] and [[Nebraska]])<br><br>'''Map legend:'''<br>{{legend|#1a3165|State or district has a Cook PVI of D+10 or greater}}{{legend|#2750b0|State or district has a Cook PVI between D+5 and D+10}}{{legend|#668be1|State or district has a Cook PVI between D+2 and D+5}}{{legend|#a8c1fa|State or district has a Cook PVI between EVEN and D+2}}{{legend|#fbb7bb|State or district has a Cook PVI between EVEN and R+2}}{{legend|#f47178|State or district has a Cook PVI between R+2 and R+5}}{{legend|#e4252f|State or district has a Cook PVI between R+5 and R+10}}{{legend|#9e151e|State or district has a Cook PVI of R+10 or greater}}]]

The '''Cook Partisan Voting Index''', abbreviated PVI or '''CPVI''', is a measurement of the partisanship of a [[List of United States congressional districts|U.S. congressional district]] or [[U.S. state]].<ref>{{cite journal | url=https://doi.org/10.1007/s00431-021-04203-y | doi=10.1007/s00431-021-04203-y | title=State-level political partisanship strongly correlates with health outcomes for US children | date=2022 | last1=Paul | first1=Megan | last2=Zhang | first2=Ruya | last3=Liu | first3=Bian | last4=Saadai | first4=Payam | last5=Coakley | first5=Brian A. | journal=European Journal of Pediatrics | volume=181 | issue=1 | pages=273–280 | pmid=34272984 | url-access=subscription }}</ref> This partisanship is indicated as lean towards either the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]] or the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]],<ref name="Cillizza 2017 i372">{{cite web | last=Cillizza | first=Chris | title=A map that shows we really do live in two different Americas | website=CNN | date=2017-04-10 | url=https://edition.cnn.com/2017/04/10/politics/map-2016-election-red-state-blue-state/index.html | access-date=2023-12-15}}</ref> compared to the nation as a whole, based on how that district or state voted in the previous two presidential elections.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.cnn.com/2018/03/14/politics/conor-lamb-pennsylvania-special-election-analysis/index.html |title=The differences between real grassroots and "Astroturf" matter |work=CNN |first=Chris|last=Cillizza |date=2018-03-14 |access-date=2020-11-29 |quote=Which brings me to the Cook Political Report's Partisan Voting Index or PVI. The goal of the PVI is to compare every congressional district to every other congressional district based on how it has performed in each of the last two presidential elections.}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.cnn.com/2018/03/14/politics/conor-lamb-pennsylvania-special-election-analysis/index.html |title=There are 119&nbsp;Republican House members who should be VERY nervous today |work=MSNBC |first=Steve|last=Benen |author-link=Steve Benen |date=2017-02-07 |access-date=2020-11-29 |quote=To get a sense of a congressional district's political leanings, there's a helpful metric called the Partisan Voter Index, or PVI, created 20&nbsp;years ago by the Cook Political Report.}}</ref>

==History==
The Partisan Voting Index was developed in 1997 by [[Charlie Cook]] of ''[[The Cook Political Report]]'', in conjunction with Clark Bensen and his political statistical analysis firm, Polidata, "as a means of providing a more accurate picture of the competitiveness".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |title=Future House Opportunities Abound (Maybe) |url=https://rollcall.com/2005/06/08/future-house-opportunities-abound-maybe/ |access-date=22 December 2023 |work=Roll Call |date=June 8, 2005}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |editor1-last=Daum |editor1-first=Courtenay |editor2-last=Duffy |editor2-first=Robert |editor3-last=Straayer |editor3-first=John |title=State of Change |date=2011 |publisher=University of Colorado Press |location=Boulder |isbn=978-1-4571-1154-9 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ncW9AwAAQBAJ |access-date=22 December 2023}}</ref><ref name="Cook" /> It is based on the methodology introduced by [[Rob Richie]] of the [[FairVote|Center for Voting and Democracy]] for the Center's July 1997 Monopoly Politics report.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cook |first1=Charlie |title=New Study Identifies 75 Seats That Should Be In Play for '98 Election |url=https://archive.fairvote.org/reports/monopoly/quotes/cook.html|access-date=18 January 2024 |work=Roll Call |date=August 11, 1997}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Monopoly Politics|url=https://archive.fairvote.org/reports/monopoly/|access-date=18 January 2024 |work=Center for Voting and Democracy |date=July 1997}}</ref>

The Cook Political Report has since released new PVI scores every two years.<ref name="Post" /> In 2021, the newsletter ended its relationship with Polidata and instead used [[Dave Leip's Atlas of U.S. Presidential Elections]] to calculate 2020's results.<ref name="Cook">{{cite web |title=The Cook Partisan Voting Index (Cook PVI) |url=https://www.cookpolitical.com/cook-pvi |website=The Cook Political Report with Amy Walter |publisher=Cook Political Report |access-date=22 December 2023}}</ref> The most recent iteration is the 2026 Cook Partisan Voting Index.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=2026-07-29 |title=Introducing the 2026 Cook PVI: Re-Redistricting Yields a More Republican, Less Elastic House Map |url=https://www.cookpolitical.com/cook-pvi/introducing-2026-cook-pvi-re-redistricting-yields-more-republican-less-elastic-house-map |access-date=2026-07-29 |website=Cook Political Report |language=en}}</ref>

==Calculation and format==
The index looks at how every congressional district voted in the past two presidential elections combined and compares it to the national average.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Seib |first1=Gerald |title=GOP Moderates and the Agony Of Their Impeachment Choice |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB913163010502936000 |access-date=22 December 2023 |work=The Wall Street Journal |date=December 9, 1998}}</ref> The Cook PVI is displayed as a letter, a plus sign, and a number, with the letter (either a D for [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] or an R for [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]]) indicating the party that outperformed in the district and the number showing how many percentage points above the national average it received.<ref name="Post">{{cite news |last1=Kane |first1=Paul |title=New report outlines the deep political polarization's slow and steady march |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/04/08/house-polarization-partisanship/ |access-date=22 December 2023 |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=April 8, 2023}}</ref> In 2022, the formula was updated to weigh the most recent presidential election more heavily than the prior election.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Wasserman |first1=David |title=Introducing the 2022 Cook Partisan Voting Index (Cook PVI) |url=https://www.cookpolitical.com/cook-pvi/2022-partisan-voting-index/introducing-2022-cook-partisan-voting-index |website=The Cook Political Report with Amy Walter |date=July 13, 2022 |access-date=2022-07-13}}</ref>

==By congressional district==
The PVIs for congressional districts are calculated based on the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020]] and [[2024 United States presidential election|2024]] presidential elections.<ref name=":0" /> With a PVI of R+3, {{ushr|PA|10}}{{update inline|reason=Missouri's Conngressional map was reverted to the one passed in 2022. MO-05 went from more Republican than PA-10 to more Democratic than PA-10. This means PA-10 is no longer the median.|date=September 2026}} was determined to be the median congressional district, meaning that exactly 217 districts are more Democratic and exactly 217 are more Republican than this district.<ref name=":0" /> {{As of|September 2026}}, 224 districts are more Republican than the national average, 204 districts are more Democratic than the national average, and 7 districts match the national average. The number of [[Marginal seat|swing seats]], defined as those between D+5 and R+5,{{Citation needed|reason=The claim that swing districts are defined as "defined as those between D+5 and R+5" is not sourced. It also seems likely that the "between" should be inclusive in this case. |date=December 2025}} is 89.<ref name=":0" />

